# mel — environment template
#
#   cp .env.example .env     # then fill in your own values
#
# HOW THESE ARE READ — important:
#   run_bot.py reads credentials from the PROCESS ENVIRONMENT first, then falls back to
#   the `.env` file NEXT TO THE CODE (load_keys). It never reads another project's .env:
#   silently borrowing a different bot's keys is how a trade lands on the wrong account.
#
#   A scheduler that supplies the environment itself works equally:
#     shell   : set -a; . /opt/data/mel/.env; set +a
#     systemd : EnvironmentFile=/opt/data/mel/.env
#     cron    : run_mel.sh sources this file when it exists
#
# .env is gitignored. Never commit real credentials.

# ── Kraken FUTURES API (futures.kraken.com — NOT spot) ───────────────────────
# Same key the account already uses for Ken. Kraken futures holds ONE position per
# symbol and the key is account-wide, so only ONE bot may be armed on this account at a
# time. Two armed bots would see each other's positions and orders.
KRAKEN_API_KEY=
KRAKEN_API_SECRET=

# ── Telegram alerts (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Create a bot with @BotFather, start a chat with it, use that chat id.
# With these blank the bot still trades; it just stays quiet.
#
# PRECEDENCE WARNING — notify.py uses a TRUTHY fallback, not "present wins":
#   token = non-empty TRIPKC_TELEGRAM_TOKEN, else TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN
#   chat  = non-empty TRIPKC_TELEGRAM_CHAT,  else TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID
# The TRIPKC_* names are inherited from Trip/bx-bot. An EMPTY TRIPKC_* value falls
# through to the matching TELEGRAM_* value. Unset both TRIPKC_* names to be certain.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=
